    <body>
      <sec id="article-31580.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Shallow water blackout, also called "hypoxic blackout," is a loss of consciousness caused by cerebral hypoxia during breath-hold submersion, most commonly following pre-dive hyperventilation. This condition often occurs in water less than 15 ft (5 m) deep but may affect swimmers or divers at greater depths. Pre-dive hyperventilation lowers arterial carbon dioxide levels, delaying the physiologic urge to breathe and allowing oxygen levels to fall to critical levels before respiratory drive is triggered. Risk factors include breath-hold diving, underwater swimming contests, competitive swimming, and unsupervised apnea diving.</p>
        <p>Affected individuals typically lose consciousness underwater without preceding distress, which may lead to drowning if rescue does not occur promptly. Diagnosis is primarily clinical and based on a history of breath-hold submersion, often with preceding hyperventilation. Management requires immediate rescue from the water, airway support, oxygenation, and resuscitation when indicated. Complications include drowning, hypoxic brain injury, and cardiac arrest. Prognosis depends on the duration of hypoxia and the timeliness of rescue.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to&#x000a0;strengthen learners' competence in evaluating and managing shallow water blackout. Participants will advance their mastery of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and evidence-based preventive, diagnostic, and therapeutic strategies. Improved skills will equip clinicians to collaborate successfully with interprofessional teams caring for at-risk and affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the etiology of shallow water blackout based on risk factors and clinical presentation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based, individualized approaches for managing&#x000a0;shallow water blackout and mitigating its potential sequelae.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve patient awareness of shallow water blackout and emphasize avoidance of hyperventilation before breath-hold submersion.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement&#x000a0;interprofessional team strategies for improving care coordination and communication to prevent and treat shallow water blackout emergencies.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=31580&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=31580">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The term "shallow water blackout" is frequently misused as a synonym for drowning, particularly when preceded by hyperventilation. Alternative terms for this condition include "underwater blackout," "underwater blackout syndrome," "sudden underwater blackout syndrome" (SUBS), "breath-holding blackout," and "free-diver blackout." A patient experiencing shallow water blackout typically loses consciousness at depths less than 15 ft (5 m), often due to prolonged breath-holding or equipment limitations. This terminology can be misleading, as drowning may occur at depths greater than 5 m.</p>
        <p>The American Red Cross, Young Men&#x02019;s Christian Association, and USA Swimming have redefined the condition as "hypoxic blackout," an&#x000a0;event described as "the loss of consciousness in the underwater swimmer or diver, during an apnea submersion preceded by hyperventilation, where alternative causes of unconsciousness have been excluded." Shallow water blackout can occur in any body of water, at any depth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r1">[1]</xref> Survival depends on prompt and effective rescue efforts, whereas delayed intervention increases the risk of fatality. Preventive measures, guided by an improved understanding of hypoxic blackout, are essential to reduce avoidable deaths.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Hypoxic blackout is a multifactorial condition. Common contributors include poor situational awareness, inadequate safety measures, and improper technique. The average person can hold their breath for 30 to 90 seconds.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r2">[2]</xref> Most hypoxic events occur when individuals attempt prolonged breath-holding that exceeds personal safety limits. Risk increases when little rest occurs between successive prolonged breath-holding attempts. Trained breath-holders can survive submerged for more than 6 minutes, but the risk of drowning remains. Drowning in otherwise healthy swimmers typically occurs during breath-holding training, competitive races, and endurance underwater swimming or diving.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r3">[3]</xref> Novice divers reaching moderate depths face the highest risk for hypoxic blackout.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Hypoxic blackout is strongly associated with hyperventilation or a series of rapid breaths immediately before submersion. This behavior constitutes the leading cause of drowning among experienced swimmers. In public aquatic settings, a lack of lifeguard awareness of hyperventilation or other high-risk behaviors substantially increases the likelihood of adverse events.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Approximately 4,000 drowning deaths occur annually in the US.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;However, the drowning death rate has declined significantly over the last 4 decades. In 1970, the rate was 3.87 deaths per 100,000 population, decreasing to 1.24 per 100,000 by 2000.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r6">[6]</xref> This decline is attributed to improved understanding of the pathophysiology and management of drowning. Despite this progress, many deaths remain preventable with relatively minimal interventions. Drowning continues to rank as the 5th leading cause of unintentional injury-related death in the US.</p>
        <p>Fatalities associated with prolonged breath-holding preceded by hyperventilation have not shown a comparable decline. Shallow water blackout occurs most frequently in male individuals younger than 40 years and carries a high fatality rate. A&#x000a0;Centers for Disease Control and Prevention case series in New York identified 16 dangerous breath-holding events, 13 involving male individuals. Four of these cases resulted in death, 3 of which involved military personnel. Between 2008 and 2015, the Marine Corps reported 24 drowning fatalities, 3 occurring on duty. During the same period, the Navy recorded 46 drowning fatalities, 14 occurring on duty.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Understanding the pathophysiology of hypoxic blackout requires first examining the physiology of diving. The underwater environment imposes increasing pressure on a diver as depth increases. These pressures are substantially higher than atmospheric pressure on land. For every additional 10 m of depth, ambient pressure rises by 1 bar or approximately 750 mm Hg.&#x000a0;High ambient pressure compresses the lungs&#x000a0;according to Boyle&#x02019;s Law, represented by the following equation:</p>
        <p>P1V1 = P2V2</p>
        <p>where P1 and V1 represent the initial pressure of the gas and lung volume at the surface, and P2 and V2 represent the pressure of the gas and lung volume at depth. This compression increases pressure within the thoracic cavity and peripheral tissues. This pressure gradient promotes central shunting of peripheral blood flow. Systemic shunting mechanisms conserve oxygen and delay the effects of hypoxia and hypercapnia. Increased intrathoracic blood volume elevates cardiac output and central venous pressure. The volume of blood displaced into the thoracic cavity is proportional to dive depth. Engorged thoracic vessels compress surrounding airspaces, further reducing lung volume via alveolar compression while increasing blood available for alveolar gas exchange.</p>
        <p>These physiological adaptations improve tolerance to hypoxia and hypercapnia. Prolonged breath-hold capacity can be achieved through structured training that allows the body to adjust to these underwater environmental stresses.</p>
        <p>Oxygen reserves become depleted after prolonged breath-holding. Oxygen deprivation combined with a suppressed or absent respiratory drive can result in unconsciousness and death. In drowning, the inability to maintain a clear airway allows water to enter the respiratory tract, either through swallowing or expulsion, which can trigger laryngospasm, promote further aspiration, and exacerbate hypoxemia, ultimately leading to unconsciousness. Hypoxic cardiac arrest follows a period of bradycardia and pulseless electrical activity. This sequence of events occurs within seconds to a few minutes.</p>
        <p>Hyperventilation before breath-holding reduces the arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide (PaCO2)&#x000a0;and delays the reemergence of the perceived need to breathe. Hyperventilation also modestly increases alveolar oxygen concentration,&#x000a0;consequently&#x000a0;raising the arterial&#x000a0;partial pressure of&#x000a0;oxygen (PaO2). However, this increase does not safely compensate for oxygen deficiency near the end of breath-holding limits.</p>
        <p>The primary urge to breathe typically occurs when the PaCO2 reaches approximately 45 to 60 mm Hg. Chemoreceptors in the medulla detect carbon dioxide levels and signal the frontal cortex to generate an irresistible impulse to breathe. Pre-dive hyperventilation lowers&#x000a0;carbon dioxide levels significantly. Thus, subsequent&#x000a0;carbon dioxide elevation during submersion may not trigger the breathing impulse before loss of consciousness from hypoxemia occurs. Arterial oxygen levels drop rapidly at the tail end of the oxygen curve. This dangerous combination of low&#x000a0;carbon dioxide drive and rapidly falling oxygen can lead to sudden unconsciousness before the breath-holder can reach the surface for air.</p>
        <p>A similar phenomenon, called "ascent blackout," can occur in deep-water diving when loss of consciousness occurs as the diver approaches the surface.&#x000a0;While performing&#x000a0;breath-hold diving, the risk of hypoxic blackout increases during ascent as arterial oxygen saturation falls below the threshold for consciousness (25&#x02013;30 mm Hg).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Unconsciousness during ascent results from reduced PaO2 due to metabolic consumption combined with decreasing ambient pressure. Reduced external pressure allows blood to redistribute to peripheral tissues. As alveoli expand from their compressed underwater state, the centrally shunted blood available for gas exchange is significantly reduced. This combination of rapidly declining gas exchange, peripheral blood redistribution, and depleted PaO2 at the end of a breath-hold substantially increases the risk of unconsciousness. Ascent blackout constitutes a form of hypoxic or shallow water blackout.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Any patient found unconscious in water requires rapid assessment and intervention. The individual should be removed from the water and placed supine on shore or in a boat.&#x000a0;After removal, the level of consciousness should be rapidly determined. Primary evaluation should then be initiated following Basic and Advanced Cardiovascular Life Support (BLS/ACLS) protocols, focusing on airway, breathing, and circulation (ABCs).&#x000a0;Resuscitation efforts should be continued concurrently with the physical examination. Prompt initiation of rescue breathing increases the likelihood of survival.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r8">[8]</xref> Emergency medical services should be notified immediately once resuscitation has begun. The patient should be transported to the nearest medical facility when stabilized. Any available medical equipment should be located and prepared by a 2nd rescuer.</p>
        <p>History and physical examination are typically brief, as priority must remain on the ABCs.&#x000a0;A situational history must be promptly obtained from bystanders when possible, including&#x000a0;family, friends, 1st-person observers, lifeguards, and swimming instructors. Information gathering should occur simultaneously with patient assessment and may guide diagnostic and treatment decisions. Additional history may be obtained after recovery of consciousness and stabilization.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Time does not allow for detailed evaluation or diagnostic studies during a water emergency. Resuscitation efforts must take priority.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Oxygenation, ventilation, and perfusion should be restored as rapidly as possible. Per BLS/ACLS protocols, responsiveness should be assessed as described above. Rescue breaths should be initiated first if the patient is not breathing. The pulse should also be evaluated. If a pulse is present, the airway and breathing portions of the algorithm should be reassessed using the jaw-thrust or tilt-chin maneuver when a head injury is suspected. If spontaneous breathing is absent, 10 breaths/min&#x000a0;should be provided, and pulses should be rechecked every 2 minutes.</p>
        <p>If no pulse is detected, cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) should be initiated for 5 cycles (approximately 2 minutes) of 30 compressions and 2 rescue breaths each. Advanced life support should be initiated early, including interventions such as advanced airway management and the use of an automated external defibrillator as equipment availability permits.</p>
        <p>Regurgitation of stomach contents is the most common complication during the resuscitation of a drowning patient. Active attempts to expel water from the airway using abdominal thrusts or head-down positioning should be avoided, as these actions delay ventilation and increase the risk of vomiting and mortality. All patients rescued from water with any loss of consciousness should undergo hospital evaluation. Most patients requiring resuscitation will require intensive care unit (ICU) admission and, in some cases, mechanical ventilation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The unexpected death of a swimmer can result from multiple causes. Evaluation of a patient who has lost consciousness in water requires consideration of all potential etiologies to guide effective intervention. Hypoxic blackout, defined as unconsciousness due to hypoxia, represents one of the most common underlying causes of morbidity and mortality in water-related emergencies. Differential diagnoses include preexisting organic cardiac disease, such as coronary artery disease or cardiomyopathy, preexisting cardiac arrhythmias, and epilepsy. These conditions may be exacerbated by the low&#x000a0;arterial oxygen saturation induced by submersion.</p>
        <p>Prolonged QT interval has also been associated with increased risk of underwater unconsciousness. Risk factors for prolonged QT include therapy with certain medications, metabolic disturbances such as hypokalemia, and alcohol use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r9">[9]</xref> During cardiac-related drowning, swimmers are often observed to stop swimming at the surface and may exhibit unusual, nonsustained behavior. In contrast, swimmers experiencing hypoxic blackout often hyperventilate before submersion and fail to resurface. Consideration of these differential diagnoses improves the effectiveness of rescue and intervention efforts.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Multiple variables determine the outcome of hypoxic blackout. Key factors include the duration of hypoxia, baseline fitness and health status, and the timeliness and quality of rescue efforts. Rescue quality depends on the training and skill of rescuers, the availability of medical supplies, and the proximity of a medical facility capable of continued stabilization and diagnostic workup following initial resuscitation.</p>
        <p>These variables make predicting individual outcomes after a hypoxic blackout challenging. Patients generally fare well when conditions are optimal, hypoxia duration is brief, and rapid, effective resuscitation is followed by prompt medical evaluation. Deficiencies in any of these parameters significantly increase the risk of death or long-term neurological deficits.</p>
        <p>Patients who achieve return of spontaneous circulation and spontaneous breathing upon hospital arrival usually recover fully. Successful CPR is associated with favorable long-term outcomes.&#x000a0;Approximately 95% of patients who regained consciousness after drowning recovered completely without complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Successful resuscitation after a hypoxic blackout requires consideration of potential complications. Four primary&#x000a0;sequelae are of greatest concern.</p>
        <p>Respiratory complications are the most common&#x000a0;complications following a drowning event. Patients are typically managed with mechanical ventilation for approximately 48 hours. This period allows regeneration of surfactant, which is washed out from the alveoli by aspirated water. Early weaning from ventilation before 48 hours may increase the risk of pulmonary edema requiring reintubation. Respiratory management follows principles similar to those used in acute respiratory distress syndrome.</p>
        <p>Pneumonia rarely results from aspirated water during a drowning incident. Pulmonary edema secondary to water aspiration is often misdiagnosed as pneumonia in the short term. Water from pools, rivers, or beaches generally does not contain sufficient bacterial load to promote pneumonia within the first 48 to 72 hours post-drowning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r10">[10]</xref> Aspirated water is typically limited in volume and rapidly absorbed into the central circulation. Prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended immediately following drowning and do not reduce the incidence of pneumonia or associated mortality.</p>
        <p>Pulmonary edema typically resolves within 3 to 4 days following a drowning incident. New or persistent pulmonary infiltrates accompanied by fever and sustained or worsening leukocytosis should be considered indicative of developing pneumonia, and antibiotic therapy should be initiated.</p>
        <p>Pneumonia following drowning most often results from aspiration of stomach contents vomited during resuscitation efforts. The risk of secondary pneumonia is comparable to ventilator-associated pneumonia, which increases to 34% to 52% by day 3 or 4 of hospitalization. Given this risk, a low threshold for initiating empiric antimicrobial therapy is reasonable in patients demonstrating systemic toxicity or hemodynamic instability without a clearly identifiable source. This threshold may be slightly lower when drowning occurs in a contaminated body of water, whether chemical or microbial.&#x000a0;However, specific treatment guidelines based on contamination type have not been established.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Cardiac complications may develop following drowning incidents. Reduced cardiac output is common immediately after a severe event and can precipitate hypotension. Initial management includes rapid crystalloid infusion, maintenance of euthermia, and optimization of oxygenation. Hypotension refractory to these measures requires initiation of vasopressors. When crystalloid resuscitation fails, further assessment of cardiac function and cardiac output with echocardiography is recommended to guide management. Use of a Swan-Ganz catheter lacks specific guidance in drowning patients and should be reserved&#x000a0;for any other&#x000a0;patient in the ICU requiring vasopressors for hypotension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Prolonged hypoxia can result in cerebral hypoxia and long-term neurological injury. Most late-term morbidity and mortality after drowning are neurologically mediated. Aggressive oxygenation during and after resuscitation is critical to prevent such outcomes. Patients remaining unresponsive after successful CPR require frequent neurological reassessment.</p>
        <p>Metabolic acidosis occurs in approximately 70% of hospitalized drowning patients and is primarily due to lactic acidosis from widespread tissue hypoxia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r13">[13]</xref> The severity of acidosis correlates with the degree of hypoxemia. Downstream consequences include acute renal impairment and multisystem organ failure. Management requires appropriate ventilator support and bicarbonate administration when pH falls below 7.2.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>The most effective strategy to reduce drowning incidence is prevention. Up to 90% of all drownings are considered preventable. Some qualified special dive operators have previously recommended hyperventilation to improve breath-holding abilities. Evidence demonstrates that hyperventilation decreases breath-holding safety and capability. The diving and medical communities must correct these misconceptions and inform the public of the associated risks. The American Red Cross, USA Swimming, and the Young Men&#x02019;s Christian Association have issued a joint statement to increase awareness of the dangers of hyperventilation before submersion breath-holding, representing a critical step in public education.</p>
        <p>Prevention in supervised locations like&#x000a0;public pools relies on prompt lifeguard recognition and response to emergent situations, including hyperventilation. Vigilant lifeguard monitoring significantly reduces the incidence of drowning-related deaths, whereas the mere presence of a lifeguard without active vigilance can provide a false sense of security and contribute to catastrophic outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31580.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Proper maintenance of swimming facilities is also essential. Water clarity must allow visualization to the bottom,&#x000a0;and pH levels&#x000a0;should&#x000a0;be&#x000a0;closely monitored. Hazards must be minimized. Public locations should ensure rapid access to rescue equipment, continuous lifeguard surveillance, and nearby phone access.</p>
        <p>Prevention of the need for rescue remains the primary goal. When rescue becomes necessary, rapid retrieval of drowning swimmers represents the only opportunity for successful resuscitation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31580.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Prevention of hypoxic blackout relies on education and correction of risky behaviors, targeting the general public, particularly individuals who swim or supervise swimmers. Relevant groups include parents, teachers, public health nurses, swim coaches,&#x000a0;scuba&#x000a0;diving instructors, and military trainers. During resuscitation, a rapid response led by a single resuscitation leader who delegates tasks maximizes efficiency and effectiveness.</p>
        <p>Postresuscitation care depends on an interprofessional team consisting of EMS personnel, emergency department staff, and ICU&#x000a0;teams to address the sequelae of a drowning incident. Prehospital and flight nurses frequently contribute to patient management. Persistent neurological deficits require specialized neurological rehabilitation. Specialty care nurses in neuroscience and rehabilitation coordinate treatment in collaboration with allied health personnel.</p>
      </sec>
